# v5.4.2-29 r2 — the fix list from round 1 (2026-11-04)

**Inputs:** `tests/v5.4.2-29/review/opus-r1.md` (ready) and `fable-r1.md` (ready). Codex did not run in round 1: its account hit its usage limit (reset 2026-11-04 18:56 CST); it reviews round 2 if Ryan keeps the three-reviewer rule for this patch.

**Agreed by both:** the data is right. Each re-downloaded the Census file (byte-identical), parsed the patch's 254 codes and names independently and re-read 49 CFR 71.7(e). No path around a rule; the load is all or nothing; the fixture moves keep every older proof; L1–L5 do not block.

Each item below is settled by a standing rule, named in brackets. Nothing goes to Ryan.

## Should fix

### S1. "Re-applying is a no-op" held only until a county was corrected (Opus S1, Fable S1; both executed)
r1 judged the rows open today. After an ordinary later correction (a zone fact closed and superseded, a county closed and succeeded, a county added from a later date), re-applying refused. After a dissolution, the insert died on the raw overlap rule (Opus N3).
**Fix (Opus's):** judge everything as of 1900-01-01, the date the load writes. The per-county lookup reads the row in force that day and its zone fact in force that day. A code with rows but none in force that day refuses with the load's own message. The postcondition says the Texas counties in force on 1900-01-01 are exactly the list, each under Texas with a zone fact of the list's zone in force that day. Fable's alternative, narrowing the header's claim, was not taken: the fix is cheap and keeps every refusal.
- Tests: reapply P8 (three later corrections, then a no-op re-apply) and P9 (a code present only from 1950). Both fail on r1. Mutations N16 (r1's "open today" lookup is back; caught at P8) and N17 (P9's refusal is gone).

[a schema object must survive what the patch says it survives; DEPLOY-VERIFICATION re-applies every patch]

### S2. The citation on the 252 new zone facts was one section off (Fable S2)
71.7(e) describes the line. That the east side is Central is 71.6(a), and that the west side is Mountain is 71.8. Source notes are never edited, so this round fixes them: `49 CFR 71.6(a), 71.7(e)` (Central) and `71.7(e), 71.8` (Mountain; no row takes it today). Battery C5 now requires the 71.6(a) citation.

[a stored citation names the provision that says it]

### S3. Mutations caught by the wording, not the behaviour (Opus S2)
Turning off the in-loop name or zone comparison still refuses, through the postcondition; the runner counted the catch by the message change. **Fix:** the patch header and the runner say which layer is which (the in-loop comparison is diagnostic, the postcondition is the guard). New mutations N14 and N15 remove the name or zone comparison from both layers; the load is then admitted and caught at P1 or P2. The author checked that N14's load exits 0.

### S4. The app-role case passed at the temp table (Opus S3)
P6 grants TEMP on its clone, so tally_app reaches `places`; it expects "permission denied for table places". P6b does the same for tally_core (Fable's probe).

### S5. Battery -16's comments named the real counties (Fable S3)
A note at the fixtures says `travis`, `harris` and `bastrop` are planted stand-ins with never-real codes. L13's comment is corrected.

## Notes taken
- **CI marker (Opus N4):** the `?` condition is now one variable (`P29_PENDING`), used by both the pending list and the gate for `reapply-29.sh`. A marker that answers neither t nor f stops the run (SETUP FAIL); it never skips silently.
- **Towns on the line (Opus):** L3 now says the line runs along the whole Texas–New Mexico border, so towns like Farwell and Texline may be Mountain by law (predicted). That is settled when cities are loaded.
- **Provenance (Opus N1):** new L6 says El Paso and Hudspeth keep -16's notes.
- **Fixture codes (Fable N6):** the header says a new fixture uses a code not on the Census list. Battery -25's 48925 is odd but beyond 48507.
- **eCFR (Fable N9):** `application/places-sources/our-sources.md` records the API URL that works.
- Not changed: C2b counts every zone fact, not only open ones (Fable N4). It holds on any database the battery runs on today, and the reapply cases cover a superseded fact.
# v5.4.2-29 r3 — the fix list from round 2 (2026-11-05)

**Input:** `tests/v5.4.2-29/review/codex-r2.md` (Codex, first round on this patch, run on Ryan's call of 2026-11-05: "run Codex on round 2"). Verdict **not yet**: one blocking item, three should-fix. The author ran all five of Codex's probes, each on a fresh clone of `tally` (with -23 and -28) with frozen r2 applied; every prediction held (results appended to `codex-r2.md`, raw output in `codex-r2-probes/results/`). Opus and Fable said ready on r1; they have not seen r2 or r3.

Each item below is settled by a standing rule, named in brackets. Nothing goes to Ryan.

## Blocking

### B1. Re-applying refused after Texas itself is closed and succeeded (Codex B1; executed, probe 02)
r2 judged each county as of 1900-01-01 but still asked for an **open** Texas. After an ordinary succession of the state (its facts and children closed, successors added from 2027, all guards on), the old Texas is still the one in force on 1900 but is closed, and the new one starts in 2027: the load refused with "no open Texas state place in force from 1900-01-01", although nothing in force on 1900 had changed.
**Fix:** Texas is the row in force on 1900-01-01 (`daterange(...) @> c_from`), as each county is. The message is now "no Texas state place in force on 1900-01-01". A county the load must still insert goes under that row; if it has closed since, the existing parent rule (-16) refuses the insert, which is right (no new open child under a closed parent).
- Tests: reapply **P10** (state succeeded after the load: re-apply is a no-op, every row unchanged) and **P10b** (state succeeded on a base without the counties: refused by the parent rule, not by the load's precondition). Both fail on frozen r2 (`[refused]`, `[refused otherwise]`). Mutation **N18** (r2's open-state lookup back) is caught at P10.

[a schema object must survive what the patch says it survives; DEPLOY-VERIFICATION re-applies every patch]

## Should fix (test-only)

### S1. A mutation counted as caught without saying how (Codex S1)
The runner took only the failing case's label, so "the mutated load applied" and "it refused for another reason" (a malformed mutation, say) looked the same. **Fix:** a failing re-apply case now says how it failed (`[admitted]`, `[refused otherwise]`, `[refused]`, `[changed]`), and each mutation names the outcome it requires: N14, N15, N9, N10 `[admitted]`; N6–N8, N12, N13, N17 `[refused otherwise]`; N16, N18 `[refused]`.

### S2. The second read of -29's CI marker could skip silently (Codex S2)
`tests/ci.sh` checked the first read (`t` or `f`, else stop) but not the second, which gates `reapply-29.sh`. **Fix:** the second read is checked the same way.

### S3. "Re-applying is a no-op" was shown by the notice, not by the data (Codex S3)
**Fix:** P7, P8 and P10 compare a digest of every `places` and `place_facts` row before and after the re-apply. P8's successors now differ from what they replace (Culberson's zone superseded by Mountain; Harris succeeded under another name, in Mountain), and Loving is closed before today with no successor.

## Notes taken
- Codex N1–N4: data, citations and the r2 fixes confirmed; no change.
- Codex N3 (a code whose only row begins after 1900, or an unfamiliar county in force on 1900, is refused rather than guessed): intended, unchanged.
- The battery is unchanged: its C-checks describe the state right after the load (r2 plan, "Not changed").

## Review of r3
The only patch change is B1, a two-line lookup change plus its message and header text. Codex reviews r3 (it raised B1). Opus and Fable, ready on r1, have not seen the r2 or r3 lookup changes; whether they re-review is for Ryan, asked with the r3 results.

