38-## 2026-11-02 (-28 r1) — percentage rates are stored as a percent to four decimals; entered and shown however the utility writes them
39-
40-- **A percentage rate is stored in percent, to four decimals; the app accepts it in any form the clerk types (percent or fraction), converts it, and shows it in the utility's preferred form (Ryan, 2026-11-02).** Round 1 of -28 (Opus S2) found the four-decimal check refuses Texas's gas gross receipts tax (Tax Code §182.022: 0.581%, 1.070%, 1.997%) and eighth-of-a-percent sales-tax pieces when they are stored as fractions (unit `decimal`: 0.01997 needs five decimals), while the same rate in percent (1.997) is accepted. *Why:* the rule is about storage; four decimals of a percent is exact for every statutory rate seen, and one stored form gives one meaning to "four decimals". A small utility's clerk who has always written fractions must not be refused or made to convert by hand: the app does the conversion. *Why not let the fraction unit carry six decimals in storage:* two stored forms of the same number, and a schedule override often takes its unit from the base item in another table, so the check would need a trigger. *Why not leave clerks to enter percent:* a refusal they cannot act on. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-45; -28's four-decimal check is unchanged, and its refusal on a `decimal`-unit rate says to store percentages in percent. Retiring the `decimal` unit is a later rates patch.
