46-## 2026-11-02 (-23 r8) — AC-44's netting: per-state before an escheat report; recorded as an application to the shortfall (-27)
47-
48-- **-27 is drafted right after -23 mirrors, ahead of -24 (Ryan, 2026-11-02).** The money stream's order becomes -23 → -27 → -24, with -25 and -26 (drafted, not reviewed) reviewed in -27's waits; then enrollments, notices, new rule types as before. *Why:* -27's row is a draw on a credit and a repayment on a payment, through -23's position and cap logic, which is freshest now and was just reviewed by the same three reviewers. *Why not keep it last:* by then that logic is cold, and -27 would likely take more rounds. *Cost:* -24 (unmatched cash, agency pledges) slips by -27's length; nothing in -24 depends on -27. -27 is a patch the 10-29 schema estimate (about 15 after -23) did not count: roughly one more. *Where it lives:* the board's patch table; `handoffs/schema-money.md` at wrap-up; the roadmap at wrap-up.
49-- **Netting a credit against a bounced payment's shortfall is recorded as a new kind of application, pointed at the payment's shortfall, built as its own patch, v5.4.2-27 (Ryan, 2026-11-02; Opus r7 N3).** AC-44 called the netting "an ordinary application", but every application names an invoice, and the shortfall is on none: a bounce waits until the payment's bill applications are undone (those bills reopen and take a credit as usual), so what is left owed is money already paid out, chiefly a refund (Kyle's 10-21 example: 50 paid, 30 refunded, 50 bounced, 30 owed). *Why:* the row records that the credit's money paid the debt, so the credit reads 0 and its dormancy clock stops, the shortfall reads what is left, and the bank tie-out stays true; it also gives R24's never-closing credit a closing act. *Why not bill the shortfall as a charge, then apply to it:* Kyle (K-10, 10-21) rejected inventing a charge that never happened ("would look like a fee the customer was never assessed, which could cause trouble with the RRC and with customers"). *Why not record nothing (the core nets only in what it shows and allows):* the account balance is already right, but the credit keeps its money and its clock, the debt never reads paid, and the database still admits the credit's refund; a change in who holds the money is stored when it happens. *Why a follow-on patch, not round 8:* the new row is a draw on the credit and a repayment on the payment, through the position and cap logic that took -23 eight rounds; it is additive (no existing column changes meaning), so it costs no data migration later. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-44; the board (-27 claimed, money stream).
50-- **Netting across payments before an escheat report is a per-jurisdiction rule the core reads, not a fixed step (Ryan, 2026-11-02; Opus r7 N3).** AC-44 had told the core to apply a credit to what the customer owes on another payment before refunding *or escheating* it. Before a refund that stands: it is Kyle's one-pot ruling (10-30) and the utility's own policy. Before an escheat report it is a setoff against a separate debt, and whether a holder may make one before reporting is each state's unclaimed-property law. *Why:* a Texas launch never justifies Texas-only design; statutory rules go in per-jurisdiction rows (rule-terms convention, 10-06). *Why not always net:* where a state bars the setoff the core would under-report, and fixing that later means reworking core logic built on it. *Why not never net:* where a state allows it, the utility remits money it could lawfully keep against a real debt. *Default where no rule row exists:* report the credit's own money in full and collect the debt separately (the conservative side). *Not checked:* no statute has been read yet; filling each state's row is the work this creates, and the schema has no per-state escheat rule table today (dormancy is hard-coded at three years in a view). *Within one payment:* not a setoff (the same money coming back); the database nets it everywhere. *Cost now:* contract wording only, no schema change. *Where it lives:* APPLICATION-CONTRACTS AC-44.
