36:- **Keep the check as built (option 1): the pipeline safety fee's state-agency exemption reads the bill's time zone for every non-zero fee under an exempting rule; load all 254 Texas counties and their zones once, as platform data, in a patch of their own (-29) (Ryan, 2026-11-04).** Round 1 of -28 (Opus S3) found that only El Paso and Hudspeth have a zone loaded, so a fee anywhere else in Texas is refused (known limit L1), and proposed reading the zone only when the agency answer differs between the earliest and latest possible midnight. Ryan: counties and their zones almost never change; it is data the database should hold once for every customer. *Why:* `places` and `place_facts` are already shared by every utility and written only by reviewed platform migrations (-16), so the cost the alternative avoided was a one-time load we owe anyway, not onboarding work per utility; with every county present the simple check never refuses for missing data, and a missing zone can never hide until a rare bill. *Why not the two-midnight shortcut:* extra logic whose only purpose was to avoid that load. *What stays per utility:* each premise's county membership — address data the utility records, needed also for city-limits sales tax (Kyle 2026-11-03 Q4); a premise with none is still refused. *Why its own patch:* -28 is mid-review; nothing is live, so -29 follows it. *Where it lives:* -28 r2 (L1 restated, no code change), -29 (Census county list; zones per 49 CFR 71.7).
405:1. **Jurisdiction = place.** A city, county, transit or special district is a place. Its taxes are separate effective-dated rows, and a service location links to every jurisdiction it sits in (`tax_jurisdictions`, gap A-8). Rates come from the Comptroller's quarterly address and rate files through a scheduled job that stages changes. No rate goes live without an operator's sign-off.
